/*!
    \page tutorial-selection-and-orders.html
    \title Tutorial 8: Selecting Units and Giving Orders
    \previouspage {Tutorial 7: The Map, Sprites and Oblique Projection}
    \nextpage {Tutorial 9: Economy, Bases and the Radial Menu}

    \section1 Selecting

    Left click selects one unit, a drag selects many. Only your own race can be selected:

    \quotefromfile qml/components/WorldMap.qml
    \skipto function isOwn(group) {
    \printuntil }

    Clicking your base selects it and keeps its menu open.

    \section1 Move or attack

    Right click on an enemy attacks it, right click on the ground moves there:

    \quotefromfile qml/components/WorldMap.qml
    \skipto const enemy = root.creatureAt(eventPoint.position, false)
    \printuntil return

    \section1 Attack move

    The \uicontrol {Attack move} toggle in the bottom bar decides what a move order does:

    \list
        \li On: units fight enemies on the way and then go on.
        \li Off: units drop their target and just walk. Use it to retreat.
    \endlist

    A unit that only walks could get stuck and be killed without fighting back. So a
    blocked unit that was hit in the last two seconds gives up the move and fights:

    \quotefromfile backend/creature.cpp
    \skipto if (game_p.move_creature_towards(id_, *destination))
    \skipto if (active_movement_goal_ == movement_goal_t::manual
    \printuntil }

    \section1 Outlines

    Selected units get a yellow outline around the sprite shape. Their target gets a red one:

    \code
    OutlineEffect {
        source: creatureSprite
        color: "#ff4a3d"
        visible: creatureView.targeted
    }
    \endcode

    \c OutlineEffect captures the source with \c ShaderEffectSource and a shader colours
    transparent pixels next to opaque ones.

*/
